  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Given that ${\\int }_{4}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3,$ calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{4}(2f(x)+x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}(2f(x)+x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{4}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(2f(x)+x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(3)\\,+\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =6\\,+\\,\\frac{16-1}{2} \\\\  & =6\\,+\\,\\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
